Because Spring built it from a `BeanDefinition`, the object is managed, proxied, and lifecycle-aware.

```java
@Component // NOT proxied for inter-bean calls
class AppConfig {
    @Bean ServiceA serviceA() { return new ServiceA(repo()); }
    @Bean Repo repo() { return new Repo(); }
    // serviceA() calls repo() directly -> a SECOND Repo instance
    // is created, bypassing the singleton cache.
}
```

```java
@Configuration // CGLIB-proxied: inter-bean calls hit the cache
class AppConfig {
    @Bean ServiceA serviceA() { return new ServiceA(repo()); }
    @Bean Repo repo() { return new Repo(); }
    // serviceA() -> repo() returns the SAME singleton Repo bean.
}
```
The CGLIB proxy on `@Configuration` is what makes inter-bean method calls return the cached singleton.

```java
@Component
@Lazy // not created at startup; built on first use
class ReportEngine {
    ReportEngine() { System.out.println("Heavy init..."); }
}

@Service
class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportEngine> engine;

    ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportEngine> engine) {
        this.engine = engine; // ReportEngine still not built here
    }

    void run() {
        engine.getObject().generate(); // built now, on first call
    }
}
```

